Supply chains for controlled pharmaceutical substances are typically **highly regulated, closed-loop, and traceable**. Structurally, they feature **licensed and vetted nodes** (API manufacturers, formulators, primary/secondary packagers, 3PLs, wholesalers, pharmacies/hospitals) with **restricted access**, **segregated storage**, and **security controls** (physical security, tamper-evident packaging, controlled transport, chain-of-custody documentation). They rely on **serialization/track-and-trace**, **inventory reconciliation**, and **auditable records** to prevent diversion, counterfeiting, and loss, often with **quota/permit constraints** and **country-specific import/export authorizations**.

Operationally, common models include:  
1) **Direct-to-licensed dispenser** (manufacturer → hospital/pharmacy) to minimize intermediaries;  
2) **Centralized wholesaler/distributor hub-and-spoke** with controlled allocation;  
3) **Specialty 3PL “secure distribution”** (dedicated lanes, monitored warehousing);  
4) **Make-to-stock with controlled allocation** or **make-to-order** for high-risk/low-volume products;  
5) **Reverse logistics** for returns, recalls, and destruction under witnessed, documented procedures.
